[{"id":"64","title":"Opening Ceremony","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Other"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"09:45","mins":"10","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"0021","name":"Catherine Sharp (moderator)"},{"id":"R0001","name":"C.E. (Catie) Murphy"},{"id":"R0002","name":"Adrian Tchaikovsky"}],"desc":"<p>Welcome to another&nbsp;<strong>Octocon<\/strong>! Come find out what we have in store for you during this year's convention, our first to be held in Cork.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"65","title":"Closing Ceremony","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Other"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"18:30","mins":"30","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"0021","name":"Catherine Sharp (moderator)"},{"id":"R0001","name":"C.E. (Catie) Murphy"},{"id":"R0002","name":"Adrian Tchaikovsky"},{"id":"R0003","name":"Premee Mohamed"},{"id":"R0004","name":"Gareth Hanrahan"},{"id":"R0005","name":"Ryan Cahill"}],"desc":"<p>Another Octocon is over... Come celebrate the best of the past year, and find out what we have lined up for 2027, including our next venue!<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"66","title":"Cosplay Parade","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Other","Tag:Cosplay","Tag:Fanfic & Transformative Works"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"14:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"Z0067","name":"Kat Dodd (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>Show off your cosplay and strut your stuff on the Octocon red* carpet in the Cosplay Parade! All ages and skill levels are welcome to participate, including commissioned and homemade costumes.<\/p>\r\n<p>Please sign up in advance at the registration desk before 1:30pm, and let us know if you need any type of accommodations - we don't have a stage so there are no ramps or steps involved.<\/p>\r\n<p>Please arrive at the Arena room (downstairs) by 2:15pm.<\/p>\r\n<p>[* Carpet is not actually red, sorry.]<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"67","title":"Interview with Catie (C.E.) Murphy","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Interview","Tag:Guest of Honour"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"17:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0040","name":"Antiqueight (moderator)"},{"id":"R0001","name":"C.E. (Catie) Murphy"}],"desc":"<p>description tbd<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"68","title":"Interview with Adrian Tchaikovsky","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Interview","Tag:Guest of Honour"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"13:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0101","name":"Fionnuala Murphy (moderator)"},{"id":"R0002","name":"Adrian Tchaikovsky"}],"desc":"<p>description tbd<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"69","title":"Interview with Premee Mohamed","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Interview","Tag:Guest of Honour"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"17:30","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0171","name":"Jonathan L. Howard (moderator)"},{"id":"R0003","name":"Premee Mohamed"}],"desc":"<p>description tbd<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"70","title":"Starship Alexandria (Live Podcast)","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Podcast","Tag:Literature","Tag:Podcasting & Audio","Tag:Guest of Honour"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0002","name":"Adrian Tchaikovsky"},{"id":"R0102","name":"Emma Newman"}],"desc":"<p>description tbd<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"71","title":"Octothorpe (Live Podcast)","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Podcast","Tag:Fandom","Tag:Podcasting & Audio"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"10:00","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0007","name":"John Coxon"},{"id":"R0008","name":"Liz Batty"},{"id":"R0009","name":"Alison Scott"}],"desc":"<p>description tbd<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"74","title":"Retelling Fairytales and Folktales","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Myths\/Legends\/Folklore","Tag:Worldbuilding","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"13:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"Z0084","name":"Liz Bourke (moderator)"},{"id":"R0001","name":"C.E. (Catie) Murphy"},{"id":"R0022","name":"Paul Carroll"},{"id":"R0087","name":"Ruth Frances Long \/Jessica Thorne"},{"id":"Z0085","name":"M.E.Moirin"}],"desc":"<p>Fairytales and folklore have been a part of literature for as long as stories have been told, but it can be tricky to tackle retelling these stories without losing their original essence. What are some of the things we love about retellings, and the things we get excited to read or watch? How do we avoid a retelling just becoming tropes, or avoid over-correcting by not using tropes at all? Our panellists are here to talk about our love of folklore and fairytales and the ways to interpret and tell these beloved stories in a new way.<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run online with different panellists.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"75","title":"Writing with Aphantasia","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Creatives","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"17:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0040","name":"Antiqueight (moderator)"},{"id":"R0001","name":"C.E. (Catie) Murphy"},{"id":"R0004","name":"Gareth Hanrahan"},{"id":"Z0088","name":"Gerald T. Mc Evoy"}],"desc":"<p>Aphantasia is the inability to create mental images in your mind's eye. When you are unable to produce these images in your mind, how do you describe things in your stories in a way that will resonate with people? It can be difficult as both a writer and a reader with aphantasia, and some would consider it a disability considering how much society leans towards the assumption that people have the ability to create vivid pictures in their mind and \"see\" the story play out while reading. Join us as we discuss the ways to handle this!<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run online with different panellists.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"76","title":"The Problem of Discoverability","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Fandom","Tag:Gaming","Tag:TV\/Film"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"10:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"R0049","name":"Tommy Ferguson (moderator)"},{"id":"R0053","name":"Declan J"},{"id":"R0063","name":"Deirdre  Thornton"},{"id":"R0077","name":"Jack-John Kirwan"}],"desc":"<p>With so many platforms and options, it can be difficult to find things you love, or things that you feel represents you. With so many demands on our already limited time, finding and choosing what we want to commit to may seem like an impossible task. Let's discuss ways to narrow down our options and find things we love again, as spread out as they may be.&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"77","title":"OH NO! The Story Changed Size!","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"14:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0066","name":"Colm Dodd (moderator)"},{"id":"R0005","name":"Ryan Cahill"},{"id":"R0052","name":"David Green"},{"id":"R0088","name":"Michael Carroll"},{"id":"R0153","name":"GT Korbin"}],"desc":"<p>You have a story planned out or in the works, but oh no! You've realized that it's not the size that you'd originally anticipated! What do you do? If it's bigger than you realized, do you condense it as much as possible or do you split it into two books? If it's smaller than you realized, how do you add more to it without it feeling superfluous? There are so many options, and it's hard to decide what the best choice is, but our panel is here to talk about their own experiences with it and answer your questions about what to do when the story you're working on no longer fits in the box.<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run online with different panellists.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"79","title":"Begosh and Begorrah, That Is Not Our Ireland","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Ireland","Tag:Worldbuilding","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"14:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0063","name":"Deirdre  Thornton (moderator)"},{"id":"R0001","name":"C.E. (Catie) Murphy"},{"id":"R0053","name":"Declan J"},{"id":"R0087","name":"Ruth Frances Long \/Jessica Thorne"},{"id":"Z0077","name":"Naomi Kelly"}],"desc":"<p>Ireland is a hugely popular setting for a lot of authors, but how familiar should they be with the reality of Ireland and not a rose-tinted nostalgic version that no longer exists (if it ever did), or with the mythology and history so they know what's suitable to use and what they can change? What is the attraction of Ireland? Why do so many non-Irish authors feel the need to use Ireland either outright as the setting or as the inspiration for the setting for their stories? It can be frustrating to see authors lauded for their creativity in coming up with a New Fantasy Language &ndash; only to look at it and realize that it's straight up Gaeilge, and the writers in question were simply not upfront about using an existing language. How do we help writers learn to do better?<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"80","title":"Beyond the Kudos: The Value of Fanfiction","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Discussion","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing","Tag:Fanfic & Transformative Works"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"17:30","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"Z0067","name":"Kat Dodd (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>Fanfiction is more than \"just a hobby\", even if writers prefer to stay in fanfiction and not step away into original fiction; it is a powerful self-sustaining literary and social ecosystem. What is the lure of fanfic, both as a creator and a reader? Is it just a need to \"fix it\" or is it to explore things that canons don't go deep enough for your tastes? What is the culture of fanfiction? Most of all, we're here to discuss the answers to a basic question: why does fanfiction matter?<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"81","title":"Graphic Violence in Media","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Comics","Tag:Literature","Tag:TV\/Film"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"19:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"Z0084","name":"Liz Bourke (moderator)"},{"id":"R0089","name":"Phaze D'Out"},{"id":"R0103","name":"E\u00f3in Dooley"},{"id":"Z0075","name":"Siobhan Murphy"}],"desc":"<p>In John Wick, the death of his beloved dog kicks off a violent rampage that spawns a full franchise. The audience empathise with the main character&rsquo;s rage despite the death occurring off-screen. Why is it that off-the-page violence can be more impactful than descriptions? How do writers use that to their advantage? How far is too far? Is there a too far when it&rsquo;s not actually on the page itself? How do you define too far when it happens off-page? How does the genre you&rsquo;re working in affect the answer to these questions?<\/p>\r\n<p>PG-13; may contain disturbing themes.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"83","title":"The Science of Science Fiction","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Science & Technology","Tag:Science Fiction","Tag:Worldbuilding"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"17:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"R0126","name":"Anatoly Belilovsky (moderator)"},{"id":"R0102","name":"Emma Newman"},{"id":"R0121","name":"Fergus Deffely"},{"id":"R0153","name":"GT Korbin"}],"desc":"<p>There seems to be an assumption in modern audiences that the science in science fiction should be accurate. In fantasy, you can do whatever you want with magic, so long as it's internally consistent, so do we have different expectations with science fiction, and are those expectations fair? With near future science fiction, such as climate fiction, audiences tend to demand more realism, so does it merely depend on the sub-genre or is there more to it? Our panel discusses the reality of science fiction and how it has changed over the years.<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run online with different panellists.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"84","title":"Satisfactory Endings","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Literature","Tag:TV\/Film"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"10:00","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0026","name":"Karina Steffens (moderator)"},{"id":"R0050","name":"RB Kelly"},{"id":"R0140","name":"Juliet E McKenna"},{"id":"R0141","name":"Farah Mendlesohn"},{"id":"Z0082","name":"Paul Anthony Shortt"}],"desc":"<p>Though the internet seems deeply divided on the endings of series such as Stranger Things, Voltron and Murderbot, plenty of people also just genuinely liked them. Writing endings is a time-honoured struggle for authors, never mind writing ones that are satisfactory. This is especially true for longer-running series, whether it&rsquo;s a book or a TV show. Even when creators have written an ending that is satisfactory within the narrative, the audience may not feel so because they&rsquo;re not ready to say goodbye or had questions the creator wasn&rsquo;t as interested in exploring as the audience. How do you go about writing a satisfactory ending to a long-running show or book?<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"87","title":"Beyond the Coffee Shop","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Fantasy","Tag:Literature","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0053","name":"Declan J (moderator)"},{"id":"R0086","name":"Gillian Polack"},{"id":"Z0068","name":"S.L. Dove Cooper"},{"id":"Z0080","name":"Bear Wiseman"}],"desc":"<p>A few years ago, cosy fantasy took publishing by apparent storm. Criticism of the genre and its popularity were not far behind and with romantasy hot on its heels, cosy fantasy looks set to be in a rut. This isn&rsquo;t, however, a full picture of the genre as it stands today. How is the genre evolving and what is its future? Have some of the strengths of cosy SFF made their way into broader genres? How is the assumption that cosy fantasy is a romantasy affecting readers and stories alike?<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"88","title":"Novellas and Where to Find Them","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Literature","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"10:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"R0063","name":"Deirdre  Thornton (moderator)"},{"id":"R0022","name":"Paul Carroll"},{"id":"R0026","name":"Karina Steffens"},{"id":"R0103","name":"E\u00f3in Dooley"},{"id":"Z0088","name":"Gerald T. Mc Evoy"}],"desc":"<p>Tor has dominated the novella awards for several years, but they are not the only publisher of novellas. In a world where shorter reads are becoming more popular, what is the field like? Where can we find novellas? Why are parts of publishing trying to avoid the word &lsquo;novella&rsquo;? Since there is a general perception that there are so few places for authors to submit novellas to, how does that affect the landscape of SFF as a whole? What can we do to celebrate novellas more? For a writer who predominantly does novellas, what changes need to happen in the publishing world to prevent their art from dying out in tradpub? Is there a stigma attached to novellas?<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"89","title":"What Have Comics Ever Done For Us?","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Comics"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"10:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"Z0067","name":"Kat Dodd (moderator)"},{"id":"R0022","name":"Paul Carroll"},{"id":"R0088","name":"Michael Carroll"},{"id":"R0121","name":"Fergus Deffely"}],"desc":"<p>There's a misconception that comics are childish, or meant for children, but for many of us, comics is how we've found pieces of ourselves, as well as people like us. Sometimes it's small moments that stick with us, and sometimes it's bigger moments that we can point to and say \"This. This is something that shaped me.\" Join us as we share the love we found in loving comics.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"90","title":"Original SF vs Adaptations on TV","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:TV\/Film"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"13:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"R0029","name":"Alex McKenzie (moderator)"},{"id":"R0022","name":"Paul Carroll"},{"id":"R0026","name":"Karina Steffens"},{"id":"R0070","name":"Clare O'Beara"},{"id":"R0089","name":"Phaze D'Out"}],"desc":"<p>It seems like nowadays most scifi shows are either adaptations from popular literary texts such as Murderbot, Foundation, or Silo, or remakes or spin-offs of original franchises such as House of Dragons, The Legend of Vox Machina, The Last of Us, or the new Star Wars and Star Trek, with other franchises looking to join in. Both Buffy and Firefly have had discussions of rebooting the shows in some form, with mixed responses. In a climate where so much is invested in reviving things, how do entirely new shows fare? What are some of the best original shows out today? With the threat of cancellation blooming over every new show, how do people take the risk to decide to watch something? Are people more drawn to adaptations because they&rsquo;re already aware of the story? Should we just remake everything with Muppets?<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"92","title":"Enabling Representation: Including Disabled Voices and Bodies in Gaming","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Gaming","Tag:Identity"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"16:00","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0028","name":"April Steenburgh (moderator)"},{"id":"R0036","name":"Finn McLellan"},{"id":"R0151","name":"Kalin Nenov (Kal)"},{"id":"Z0082","name":"Paul Anthony Shortt"}],"desc":"<p>We are increasingly talking about the accessibility of games, both video and TTRPG. But what about disability in the games themselves? Representation in secondary characters and NPCs is important but fosters the impression that people with disabilities can only have supporting roles. What do we need for people to realise that disabled people can be heroes, too, without objectifying them?<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"93","title":"Indie Board Games: Where to Start?","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Gaming"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"14:30","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0028","name":"April Steenburgh (moderator)"},{"id":"R0007","name":"John Coxon"},{"id":"R0009","name":"Alison Scott"},{"id":"Z0080","name":"Bear Wiseman"}],"desc":"<p>What does the board game landscape look like for people, particularly if you want to play something outside of games published by Hasbro or Ravensburger? There are a lot of new play styles emerging from interesting kickstarters, but where to actually start can be a problem. Join us as we discuss this, and try to provide answers to your questions!<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"95","title":"What\u2019s in a Name?","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Worldbuilding","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"16:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"R0088","name":"Michael Carroll (moderator)"},{"id":"R0002","name":"Adrian Tchaikovsky"},{"id":"R0087","name":"Ruth Frances Long \/Jessica Thorne"},{"id":"Z0077","name":"Naomi Kelly"}],"desc":"<p>One of the most delightful (or terrible for some creators) parts of writing is naming things, whether that&rsquo;s a character, a place, an item or something else. But how do you find the perfect name? We're here to share our own stories, the processes we use, and the laughs along the way.<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run online with different panellists.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"99","title":"The Outer Limits: Humanity in SF","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Science Fiction"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"10:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0049","name":"Tommy Ferguson (moderator)"},{"id":"R0001","name":"C.E. (Catie) Murphy"},{"id":"R0002","name":"Adrian Tchaikovsky"},{"id":"R0073","name":"Val Nolan"},{"id":"Z0084","name":"Liz Bourke"}],"desc":"<p>What is it about the far reaches of space that brings readers back to sci-fi and where do we go from here? Sometimes there's a draw to the soft science parts of sci-fi because we don't have to worry about it becoming reality and that makes it safe to explore new worlds and creatures. Join our panel to talk about the joy and fear of the far reaches of space, how we use sci-fi to talk about our own world, and why aliens and sentient spaceships often appear the most human.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"102","title":"The Importance of Human Created Work in This Age of AI Slop","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Science & Technology","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"14:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"R0134","name":"Edmund Schluessel (moderator)"},{"id":"R0029","name":"Alex McKenzie"},{"id":"R0077","name":"Jack-John Kirwan"},{"id":"R0102","name":"Emma Newman"},{"id":"R0149","name":"Jonny Nexus"}],"desc":"<p>Sometimes people get accused of using genAI when they aren&rsquo;t &ndash; and it seems like anyone perceived as \"other\" gets targeted by those accusations. How do we deal with the possibility where anyone can accuse you of using genAI without any proof? Tools like Ellipsus and Inkbreaker have created ways to \"prove\" that our work is human-generated, which has led to large backlash among the writers using it when it was introduced, since they can't <strong>actually<\/strong> prove anything and are logging keystrokes. What are people using as proof of AI, and is it one singular thing or an aggregate that comes together? Most importantly, though, we want to talk about the need to continue creating things ourselves, as imperfect as they may be. After all, to err is human.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"103","title":"Cosplayers Unite","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Discussion","Tag:Cosplay","Tag:Crafting & Making","Tag:Fanfic & Transformative Works"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"13:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 2&3"],"people":[{"id":"R0026","name":"Karina Steffens (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>Come talk to each other about your cosplays, how you did them, the work you put into them, and the little extras that go a long way. Talk about the cosplays you loved and the ones that seemed like you'd never get them right. Talk about the cosplays you want to do, and what inspires you. Talk about your love of cosplay with other people that also love cosplay!<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"104","title":"Speculative Fiction in Translation: Small Cultures and Large Themes","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Literature","Tag:Translation","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0151","name":"Kalin Nenov (Kal) (moderator)"},{"id":"R0011","name":"Jean B\u00fcrlesk"},{"id":"R0086","name":"Gillian Polack"},{"id":"R0126","name":"Anatoly Belilovsky"},{"id":"R0172","name":"Keren Landsman"}],"desc":"<p>In recent years, English language speculative fiction has visibly opened up to writers from all around the world, whether through these authors writing in English or through translation. Translating from \"smaller\" languages into English has become more common, and subsequently finds a larger audience. But some questions remain across cultures and languages: why are we here? where are we going? what can we yet become?<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"105","title":"When Do We Kill the Horror Franchise (and Make It Stay Dead)?","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Horror","Tag:TV\/Film"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"17:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"Z0031","name":"MaryBrigid Turner (moderator)"},{"id":"R0022","name":"Paul Carroll"},{"id":"R0052","name":"David Green"},{"id":"R0062","name":"David Bamford"},{"id":"Z0067","name":"Kat Dodd"}],"desc":"<p>There are thirteen Halloween movies, nine movies in the Conjuring universe, nine Nightmares on Elm Street, seven Resident Evil movies, five installments in the 28 Days Later franchise, seven Scream movies and a television show, five Insidious movies, and thirteen seasons of American Horror Story. These are big numbers, but are they used to their best advantage or are some of those movies just padding that don't reach their full potential? What is it about long-running horror series (in both television and film) that intrigues us? What makes it work? Where do things get messy? Most importantly: are the audiences leaving these satisfied with what they're receiving now? Join us as we discuss Horror and whether a franchise needs to die and stay dead, or return in yet another sequel.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"106","title":"A History of Anime","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Anime"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"16:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0026","name":"Karina Steffens (moderator)"},{"id":"R0077","name":"Jack-John Kirwan"},{"id":"R0155","name":"Fi"},{"id":"Z0088","name":"Gerald T. Mc Evoy"}],"desc":"<p>Over the years, Anime has become more prevalent in the Western world, and talked about more openly. It's no longer seen as a niche interest, but some knowledge <strong>is<\/strong> still niche, and we want to talk about some of that now. All art is living and breathing, and it adapts and changes over the years. Join us as we talk about where Anime started, where it's gone, where it currently is, and where we hope it goes in the future!&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"107","title":"Rooting for the Anti-Hero","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Comics","Tag:Literature","Tag:TV\/Film","Tag:Worldbuilding"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"Z0067","name":"Kat Dodd (moderator)"},{"id":"R0004","name":"Gareth Hanrahan"},{"id":"R0088","name":"Michael Carroll"},{"id":"Z0084","name":"Liz Bourke"},{"id":"Z0085","name":"M.E.Moirin"}],"desc":"<p>Morally gray, or deeply flawed, characters can be interesting and raise some profound questions. People love to dig into villains, but what about the anti-heroes? The ones who know that they aren't heroes, but see their actions as necessary. Join us as we dig into these characters and why they do what they do &ndash; and why we love them for it.<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run online with different panellists.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"110","title":"Beyond 5E: What\u2019s happening in the Tabletop Sphere?","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Gaming"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"13:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"Z0088","name":"Gerald T. Mc Evoy (moderator)"},{"id":"R0004","name":"Gareth Hanrahan"},{"id":"R0029","name":"Alex McKenzie"},{"id":"R0054","name":"Brian Nisbet"},{"id":"R0089","name":"Phaze D'Out"}],"desc":"<p>For years, TTRPGs have been dominated by Wizards of the Coast with D&amp;D, partially because of the popularity of the system, and partially because of their Open Game Licences that allowed people to use and modify the system. Their recent decision to change these licences has severely impacted the wider gaming community, especially smaller or more diverse developers who'd created more inclusive gameplay options. Our panel explores where tabletop roleplaying currently is in the aftermath, and where it can go from here.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"111","title":"Crowd-funding Your Creativity","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Creatives","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0040","name":"Antiqueight (moderator)"},{"id":"R0001","name":"C.E. (Catie) Murphy"},{"id":"R0005","name":"Ryan Cahill"},{"id":"R0022","name":"Paul Carroll"},{"id":"R0126","name":"Anatoly Belilovsky"}],"desc":"<p>If you build it, will they come? Crowdfunding has become a staple of many creators&rsquo; lives, providing opportunities for creating books, comics and games that wouldn&rsquo;t otherwise exist. From Kickstarter campaigns and Patreon communities, and the burgeoning world of web comics, our panel of creators share their experiences in finding supportive audiences online and occasionally getting them to part with some cash. Some incentives required.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"113","title":"Don't Go Alone: The Importance of Writing Support Networks","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Creatives","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"Z0088","name":"Gerald T. Mc Evoy (moderator)"},{"id":"R0001","name":"C.E. (Catie) Murphy"},{"id":"R0097","name":"Philippa Ryder"},{"id":"R0103","name":"E\u00f3in Dooley"},{"id":"Z0075","name":"Siobhan Murphy"}],"desc":"<p>Writing may seem a solitary activity, but every Frodo needs their Sam (and the rest of the Fellowship!) to keep them from struggling alone. Sometimes we need to cry about how our characters are breaking our hearts, sometimes we need to scream because our characters won't listen to us and do what we need them to do, and sometimes we need advice or a sense-check. Our panel of writers talk about the support they get from their writing communities.<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run online with different panellists.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"114","title":"World-building for Realism","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Worldbuilding"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"16:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 4 \/ Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"0021","name":"Catherine Sharp (moderator)"},{"id":"R0002","name":"Adrian Tchaikovsky"},{"id":"R0004","name":"Gareth Hanrahan"},{"id":"R0087","name":"Ruth Frances Long \/Jessica Thorne"},{"id":"R0102","name":"Emma Newman"}],"desc":"<p>Descriptions of everyday objects, clothing and food can bring an imagined world to life: establishing culture, revealing inspiration, and depicting settings through the smallest of details. What elements can you include to make a world feel truly lived in and as though its fictional cultures could really exist, and how can you avoid overwhelming the story and characters with your world-building?<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run online with different panellists.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"119","title":"Workshop: Beat Procrastination and Write that Book!","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Workshop","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Tucana"],"people":[{"id":"R0102","name":"Emma Newman (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>Are you struggling to write the first draft of your novel? Are you researching endlessly but not actually writing the book? Have you had a book in your head for years but been unable to start it? Do you have a drawer full of unfinished stories and manuscripts? If so, this workshop with Emma Newman can help. It&rsquo;s designed to help you explore the reasons why you're not getting that book written with practical tasks designed to help you start and finish that manuscript lurking at the back of your mind. (Bring a pen and paper!)<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"120","title":"The Latest Greatest Octocon Table Quiz","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Other","Tag:Fun Stuff"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"20:00","mins":"120","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"0021","name":"Catherine Sharp (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>Here we go again for another table quiz to raise money for our chosen charity - this year, it's <a href=\"https:\/\/corkpennydinners.org\">Cork Penny Dinners<\/a>!<\/p>\r\n<p>Come and test your knowledge on all things science fiction, fantasy and horror, books, TV, film and comics in the Octocon Table Quiz, and maybe even win a prize*.<br \/><br \/>Teams of 4(ish); &euro;5 per team member as a donation to Octocon's chosen charity. Sign up in advance at the registration desk, or just show up with your teammates. Note that space is limited so you might end up sharing a table with other teams.<br \/><br \/>* Prizes are presented per team (and are based on teams of 4); there's no guarantee on the quality of prizes.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"121","title":"Karaoke Time (sponsored by Dublin 2029)","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Other","Tag:Fun Stuff"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"22:00","mins":"120","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":null,"desc":"<p>Come along to our karaoke session, sponsored by the <a href=\"https:\/\/dublin2029.ie\">Dublin 2029 Worldcon bid<\/a>.<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\r\n<p>(Note that this might start later than 21:30 if the quiz runs long. Or might start earlier!)<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"124","title":"From Flight to Fight","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Talk","Tag:Fantasy","Tag:Horror"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"13:00","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"Z0082","name":"Paul Anthony Shortt (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>Paul Anthony Shortt discusses the connections between horror and fantasy genres and how, over time, any given horror storyline will eventually evolve into fantasy. He will look at the direct impact of how horror protagonists develop in skill and agency over time, and also at thematic matters such as how the key aspect of horror is a loss or lack of control on the part of the characters, whereas a key aspect of fantasy is that the characters have the power to enact their will upon the environment around them.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"125","title":"Wicked and the Heroine's Journey","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Talk"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"16:00","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"Z0087","name":"Valerie Estelle Frankel (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>The Wicked stage musical and film stand out for their double journey&mdash;two heroines growing through near-opposite paths, in a film industry that rarely focuses on young women&rsquo;s friendships. Elphaba turns toward darkness, straddling a dark line. Still, her MGM iconography is subtly subverted with goddess nature imagery. When Glinda, the villainess&rsquo;s good shadow, enters the witch&rsquo;s stronghold and reasons with her, she pulls Elphaba back from despair. Accordingly, Elphaba makes her her heir, in a film ending that offers a glimmer of light in the future. With this, Elphaba retires to priestess, leaving her counterpart to rule.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"127","title":"Getting Younger People Reading","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Talk"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"Z0074","name":"Mark Kielty (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"128","title":"Sustainable Cosplay","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Talk","Tag:Cosplay","Tag:Crafting & Making"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"10:00","mins":"30","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"Z0067","name":"Kat Dodd (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"129","title":"Cosplay Fails","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Talk","Tag:Cosplay","Tag:Crafting & Making"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"10:30","mins":"30","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"Z0067","name":"Kat Dodd (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"130","title":"Meet the Author: Adrian Tchaikovsky","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Meet the Creator","Tag:Guest of Honour"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"14:30","mins":"60","loc":["Tucana"],"people":[{"id":"R0002","name":"Adrian Tchaikovsky"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"131","title":"Meet the Author: C.E. (Catie) Murphy","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Meet the Creator","Tag:Guest of Honour"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"14:30","mins":"60","loc":["Tucana"],"people":[{"id":"R0001","name":"C.E. (Catie) Murphy"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"132","title":"Meet the Author: Premee Mohamed","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Meet the Creator","Tag:Guest of Honour"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"19:00","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"R0003","name":"Premee Mohamed"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"133","title":"Meet the Author: Gareth Hanrahan","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Meet the Creator"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Tucana"],"people":[{"id":"R0004","name":"Gareth Hanrahan"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"134","title":"Meet the Author: Ryan Cahill","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Meet the Creator"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"17:30","mins":"60","loc":["Tucana"],"people":[{"id":"R0005","name":"Ryan Cahill"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"135","title":"Meet the Author: S.M. Hallow","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Meet the Creator"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"16:00","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"R0010","name":"S.M. Hallow"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"136","title":"Meet the Author: Jean B\u00fcrlesk","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Meet the Creator"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"R0011","name":"Jean B\u00fcrlesk"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"137","title":"Reading: Emma Newman","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Reading"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"19:00","mins":"30","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"R0102","name":"Emma Newman"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"138","title":"Meet the Podcasters: Octothorpe","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Meet the Creator"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"13:00","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"R0007","name":"John Coxon"},{"id":"R0008","name":"Liz Batty"},{"id":"R0009","name":"Alison Scott"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"139","title":"Affinity Space: LGBTQIA+","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Discussion","Tag:Identity"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"14:30","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":null,"desc":"<p>Private affinity space<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"140","title":"Affinity Space: Disability","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Discussion","Tag:Identity"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"14:30","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":null,"desc":"<p>Affinity space<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"141","title":"Don't Go Alone: The Importance of Writing Support Networks (Online)","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Creatives","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"13:00","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0151","name":"Kalin Nenov (Kal) (moderator)"},{"id":"R0117","name":"Sarah Johnson"},{"id":"R0140","name":"Juliet E McKenna"},{"id":"R0172","name":"Keren Landsman"},{"id":"Z0068","name":"S.L. Dove Cooper"}],"desc":"<p>Writing may seem a solitary activity, but every Frodo needs their Sam (and the rest of the Fellowship!) to keep them from struggling alone. Sometimes we need to cry about how our characters are breaking our hearts, sometimes we need to scream because our characters won't listen to us and do what we need them to do, and sometimes we need advice or a sense-check. Our panel of writers talk about the support they get from their writing communities.<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run in person with different panellists.<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"143","title":"Representation: Not A One Size Fits All","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Creatives","Tag:Fandom","Tag:Identity"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"13:00","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"Z0067","name":"Kat Dodd (moderator)"},{"id":"R0006","name":"Ruth Ennis"},{"id":"R0036","name":"Finn McLellan"},{"id":"R0151","name":"Kalin Nenov (Kal)"},{"id":"R0170","name":"Mona Tewari"}],"desc":"<p>People are different and, thus, are affected differently by their neurodiversity, their disability, their ethnicity, and their sexual or romantic orientation. It can be frustrating to see people complain about something because \"that's not how it affects me, therefore it's incorrect\" on things that are different for every person, so how do we deal with that without letting it drag us down? Do creators have to \"out\" themselves as dealing with these things themselves in order to be taken seriously, and is that fair to ask of them? But equally do we, as an audience member, need to out ourselves in order to be taken seriously in our concerns, and is that fair to ask of us?<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run in person with different panellists.<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"144","title":"Retelling Fairytales and Folktales (Online)","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Myths\/Legends\/Folklore","Tag:Worldbuilding","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"14:30","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0140","name":"Juliet E McKenna (moderator)"},{"id":"R0006","name":"Ruth Ennis"},{"id":"R0010","name":"S.M. Hallow"},{"id":"R0011","name":"Jean B\u00fcrlesk"},{"id":"R0028","name":"April Steenburgh"}],"desc":"<p>Fairytales and folklore have been a part of literature for as long as stories have been told, but it can be tricky to tackle retelling these stories without losing their original essence. What are some of the things we love about retellings, and the things we get excited to read or watch? How do we avoid a retelling just becoming tropes, or avoid over-correcting by not using tropes at all? Our panellists are here to talk about our love of folklore and fairytales and the ways to interpret and tell these beloved stories in a new way.<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run in person with different panellists.<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"145","title":"The Science of Science Fiction (Online)","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Science & Technology","Tag:Science Fiction","Tag:Worldbuilding"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"17:30","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0114","name":"Mark Mullan (moderator)"},{"id":"R0003","name":"Premee Mohamed"},{"id":"R0007","name":"John Coxon"},{"id":"R0134","name":"Edmund Schluessel"},{"id":"R0172","name":"Keren Landsman"}],"desc":"<p>There seems to be an assumption in modern audiences that the science in science fiction should be accurate. In fantasy, you can do whatever you want with magic, so long as it's internally consistent, so do we have different expectations with science fiction, and are those expectations fair? With near future science fiction, such as climate fiction, audiences tend to demand more realism, so does it merely depend on the sub-genre or is there more to it? Our panel discusses the reality of science fiction and how it has changed over the years.<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run in person with different panellists.<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"146","title":"What\u2019s in a Name? (Online)","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Worldbuilding","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"20:30","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0003","name":"Premee Mohamed"},{"id":"R0011","name":"Jean B\u00fcrlesk"},{"id":"R0050","name":"RB Kelly"},{"id":"R0170","name":"Mona Tewari"},{"id":"Z0080","name":"Bear Wiseman"}],"desc":"<p>One of the most delightful (or terrible for some creators) parts of writing is naming things, whether that&rsquo;s a character, a place, an item or something else. But how do you find the perfect name? We're here to share our own stories, the processes we use, and the laughs along the way.<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run in person with different panellists.<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"147","title":"World-building for Realism (Online)","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Worldbuilding"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"16:00","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"R0141","name":"Farah Mendlesohn (moderator)"},{"id":"R0003","name":"Premee Mohamed"},{"id":"R0006","name":"Ruth Ennis"},{"id":"R0050","name":"RB Kelly"},{"id":"R0170","name":"Mona Tewari"}],"desc":"<p>Descriptions of everyday objects, clothing and food can bring an imagined world to life: establishing culture, revealing inspiration, and depicting settings through the smallest of details. What elements can you include to make a world feel truly lived in and as though its fictional cultures could really exist, and how can you avoid overwhelming the story and characters with your world-building?<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run in person with different panellists.<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"149","title":"Rooting for the Anti-Hero (Online)","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Panel","Tag:Comics","Tag:Literature","Tag:TV\/Film","Tag:Worldbuilding"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"19:00","mins":"60","loc":["Streamed in Member Portal"],"people":[{"id":"Z0081","name":"Vanessa MacLaren-Wray (moderator)"},{"id":"R0010","name":"S.M. Hallow"},{"id":"R0011","name":"Jean B\u00fcrlesk"},{"id":"R0036","name":"Finn McLellan"},{"id":"Z0087","name":"Valerie Estelle Frankel"}],"desc":"<p>Morally gray, or deeply flawed, characters can be interesting and raise some profound questions. People love to dig into villains, but what about the anti-heroes? The ones who know that they aren't heroes, but see their actions as necessary. Join us as we dig into these characters and why they do what they do &ndash; and why we love them for it.<\/p>\r\n<p>This panel is also being run in person with different panellists.<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"150","title":"Workshop: Worldbuilding","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Workshop"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"10:00","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"R0086","name":"Gillian Polack (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"151","title":"Workshop: Writing Speculative Poetry","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Workshop","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"17:30","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"Z0081","name":"Vanessa MacLaren-Wray (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"152","title":"Workshop: Developmental Editing","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Workshop","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"10:00","mins":"60","loc":["Tucana"],"people":[{"id":"Z0075","name":"Siobhan Murphy (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"153","title":"Workshop: Get Started in Comics","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Workshop","Tag:Comics","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"16:00","mins":"60","loc":["Tucana"],"people":[{"id":"R0022","name":"Paul Carroll (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>There's no one path in comics that fits all, but the building blocks are all the same. Join comic writer and award-winning editor Paul Carroll in an hour-long journey to demystify the process of writing comics and getting a start in the industry.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"154","title":"Workshop: Found Footage Formats","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Workshop","Tag:Writing\/Editing\/Publishing"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"13:00","mins":"60","loc":["Tucana"],"people":[{"id":"R0153","name":"GT Korbin (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"155","title":"Workshop: Representation in Wikipedia","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Workshop","Tag:Creatives","Tag:Fandom"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"10:00","mins":"60","loc":["Tucana"],"people":[{"id":"R0040","name":"Antiqueight (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"156","title":"Discussion: The Troubles and Thoughtscapes of \"Good\" Characters","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Discussion"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"10:00","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"R0151","name":"Kalin Nenov (Kal) (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>Let's take a deep look at what we call \"good\" characters. The ones who genuinely strive to understand everyone and make things better so they can get some sleep at night; who sacrifice no-one but themselves or know better than to see their acts as \"sacrifice\". What troubles them? What makes them multilayered? Where did we find them last?<\/p>\r\n<p>This discussion is open to all, and is the nemesis&mdash;ahem&mdash;complement to the <em>Rooting for the Anti-Hero<\/em> panel.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"158","title":"The Soul Atlas Universe","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Talk","Tag:Creatives","Tag:Fantasy","Tag:Worldbuilding"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"16:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"Z0078","name":"Sofiia Sarantseva (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>The &ldquo;Soul Atlas&rdquo; is a fantasy universe inspired by places like \"Middle Earth\" or \"Westeros\", with several projects already made (a short film \"Leilani\" which will debut next year and &ldquo;Hope&rdquo; in development), several music albums (\"The Conference\", \"Agaiatar Legends\", \"Journey to Agaiatar\", \"The Anomaly\"), a board game (\"Trials of Akash\") and now a comic book &ldquo;Grimhald&rdquo;!<br \/><br \/>The Soul Atlas company is registered in Ireland, and the projects are being created and developed by a collaborative of Irish and other international artists (more than 200 artists from various media), creating a high fantasy universe!&nbsp; You can see it on <a href=\"https:\/\/soul-atlas.com\/\">the Soul Atlas website<\/a>.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"159","title":"The Annual Crochet-an-Octo Festival","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Workshop","Tag:Crafting & Making"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"13:00","mins":"60","loc":["Tucana"],"people":[{"id":"Z0031","name":"MaryBrigid Turner (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"163","title":"Reading: David Green","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Reading"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"19:30","mins":"30","loc":["Pegasus 1"],"people":[{"id":"R0052","name":"David Green"}],"desc":"<p>reading slot<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"165","title":"Play D.A.M.N.I.T.","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Scheduled Game","Tag:Fun Stuff","Tag:Gaming"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"15:30","mins":"120","loc":["Pegasus 2&3"],"people":[{"id":"Z0088","name":"Gerald T. Mc Evoy (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>In D.A.M.N.I.T. (Dwarven Assault Mining Neutralization and Interception Team) you play special forces Dwarves in space, a rapid reaction force deployed to protect people (though mainly dwarves) from pirates, beasties and other threats on the galactic fringe.<br \/>Brought to you by the minds behind Pew Pew, Cocaine Owlbear and other assorted silliness.<\/p>\r\n<p>Maximum of 5 players using pregenerated characters. Sign-ups required.<\/p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"166","title":"Play Jackbox Games","tags":["Track:Online only","Division:Programming","Type:Scheduled Game","Tag:Gaming"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"20:30","mins":"60","loc":["Zoom"],"people":[{"id":"Z0087","name":"Valerie Estelle Frankel (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>Jackbox games<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"167","title":"Octocon 2026 Annual General Meeting","tags":["Track:In-person + streamed","Division:Programming","Type:Meeting"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"16:00","mins":"60","loc":["Tucana"],"people":[{"id":"0021","name":"Catherine Sharp (moderator)"},{"id":"R0029","name":"Alex McKenzie"},{"id":"Z0067","name":"Kat Dodd"}],"desc":"<p>Octocon AGM<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"168","title":"Wings and Things","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Workshop","Tag:Crafting & Making","Tag:Children's"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 2&3"],"people":null,"desc":"<p>Wings and tails and ears, oh my! Come create some fun cosplay staples.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"169","title":"Make a Bookmark","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Workshop","Tag:Crafting & Making","Tag:Children's"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"14:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 2&3"],"people":null,"desc":"<p>Create and decorate a bookmark<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"172","title":"Play Discworld: Ankh Morpork (session 1)","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Scheduled Game","Tag:Fun Stuff","Tag:Gaming"],"date":"2026-10-03","time":"20:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 2&3"],"people":[{"id":"R0089","name":"Phaze D'Out (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>Come play the Discworld: Ankh Morpork boardgame! Maximum 4 players.&nbsp;<br \/>Show up early if you want to be sure of a game.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"173","title":"Play Discworld: Ankh Morpork (session 2)","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Scheduled Game","Tag:Fun Stuff","Tag:Gaming"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"16:00","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 2&3"],"people":[{"id":"R0089","name":"Phaze D'Out (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>Come play the Discworld: Ankh Morpork boardgame! Maximum 4 players.<\/p>\r\n<p>Show up early if you want to be sure of a game.<\/p>","links":[]},{"id":"174","title":"Fun with Folds: Origami Workshop","tags":["Track:In-person only","Division:Programming","Type:Workshop","Tag:Crafting & Making","Tag:Children's"],"date":"2026-10-04","time":"11:30","mins":"60","loc":["Pegasus 2&3"],"people":[{"id":"R0155","name":"Fi (moderator)"}],"desc":"<p>[description tbc]<\/p>","links":[]}]